{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"agent_message","text":"These are three distinct layers:\n\n- **Transparency policy:** The UCITS management company must describe its ETF disclosure arrangements—what is disclosed, when and where, and how the arrangements support pricing and arbitrage. A policy disclosure is not itself disclosure of the portfolio. CBI Q&A 1012 (April 2025) addresses this policy obligation.\n- **Daily public holdings:** The ordinary route is to publish the ETF’s portfolio holdings daily, with the information available to market participants. This is an operational disclosure requirement, not merely a statement of policy.\n- **Conditional periodic-holdings route:** The 2026 rules allow less-frequent public portfolio disclosure only where the specified conditions are met. In that case, the ETF must still provide daily information to authorised participants and market makers sufficient to facilitate effective arbitrage, while protecting that information from wider public disclosure.\n\nSo, “transparency policy” does not mean “daily holdings,” and periodic public holdings do not eliminate the daily arbitrage-information safeguard."}}
